description:
FILED s515a (Opus, 2026-10-11) from #3185's edge probes.  PRE-EXISTING (same output on s514f's tip 8c683ae6).

`local *STDOUT` does not localize the glob's IO slot (tie-aggregates.md section 7: only a tie PLACEHOLDER made inside the scope is undone), so a tie of an OPENED handle inside `local *STDOUT` keys on the real STDOUT stream and SURVIVES the scope: every later print goes to the tie object.

    package Obj; sub TIEHANDLE { bless {}, shift } sub PRINT { 1 }
    package main;
    { local *STDOUT; tie *STDOUT, 'Obj'; print "hidden\n"; }
    print "end\n";

perl 5.40.3: `end`.   PCL: nothing (silent wrong; rc 0).

Fix direction: %p-glob-restore-tied-io (the local *FH undo) should also drop a tie record made inside the scope on a stream that was the glob's IO at the save (record the tie's owner glob + a scope serial), or `local *FH` should localize the IO slot for real (perl does: inside the scope *STDOUT has no IO at all, so the tie gets a placeholder).  Probe the Capture::Tiny `local *STDOUT` shapes before choosing.


=== DONE (s518d, commit e4a878df) ===
cl/pcl-runtime.lisp: a BARE `local *STDIN` / `*STDOUT` / `*STDERR` (p-local-glob, p-local-glob-if, p-local-glob-dynamic with no value) gives the scope a handle with NO IO and restores it at the end (%p-std-local-enter / %p-std-local-leave): the handle-table entry -- and for STDIN/STDOUT the default CL stream -- is a CLOSED stream in the scope (print false, nothing written, tied false); a tie made in the scope keys on that stream and is dropped at the end; an open in the scope moves onto descriptor 0/1/2 as every standard-handle open does, and the end dup2s a saved dup of the descriptor back and puts the old streams + table entry (and so the outer tie) back.  STDERR's CL stream is left alone (perl's warn/die reach the real stderr).  %p-out-fh-or-fail asks the tie before the writable test (a tie keyed on the closed in-scope stream answers print through its object).  The reproducer prints `end` = perl; the in-scope prints reach PRINT as in perl.  Residue: a fileno read inside the scope after an open is 1 where perl's fresh open is 3 (ir-spec 2.4b says so).  Guard Pl/t/tiehandle-01.t rows 20-22, inverse-verified on main.  ir-spec 2.4b; tie-aggregates.md section 7.
